# 003_diploid_baseline — within-species diploid divergence vs. Cbp (inventory B1 #9)

Reuses `../002_alignment/results/03_summary/pair_metrics.tsv` (no new alignment).
Asks what "normal" divergence between diploid accessions looks like, to judge
whether Cbp-subgenome vs diploid-donor divergence is unusual.

    cd analyses/003_diploid_baseline
    python3 scripts/000_baseline.py     # plain python3; writes results/

Outputs (`results/`, gitignored): `pair_baseline.tsv` (per pair: SNP/kb and
indel/kb over syntenic reference bp, large SV (INV+TRANS+DUP rows)/Mb,
min syntenic fraction of the two genomes) and `class_summary.tsv` (median/min/max
per subgenome set x class: diploid-diploid, Cbp-diploid, Cbp-Cbp).

## First look (preliminary, descriptive only)
SNP/kb, median (range):

| set | diploid-diploid | Cbp-diploid | Cbp-Cbp |
|---|---|---|---|
| Co (Co39, CoMosher, CoRussian) | 0.9 (0.8-1.2) | 5.1 (4.0-14.2) | 8.3 (3.0-9.4) |
| Cr (Cr145, CrMosher, CgMosher) | 8.9 (4.2-13.6) | 9.3 (7.0-18.1) | 9.2 (3.7-9.9) |

- Co: Cbp's Co subgenome is ~4-5x more diverged from the Co diploids than the
  Co diploids are from each other. CbpChinese drives the high end (10-14).
- Cr: the "diploid" baseline already spans 4-14 SNP/kb because it includes
  CgMosher (*C. grandiflora*, outcrosser; Cr descends from Cg), so Cbp-vs-diploid
  is within the diploid range. Cr145 vs CrMosher alone (4.2) is the within-species
  baseline; Cg pairs are the more relevant comparison for the Cr progenitor.
- Caveats: n=3 pairs per class, pairs are not independent (shared genomes);
  the denominator is syntenic bp (aligned sequence only, so divergent regions
  are under-represented); Co within-species pairs may be closely related
  accessions; assembly differences (CoMosher 181 Mb vs Co39 135 Mb) affect SV counts.
